{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Moses was on the mountain.</b> Every time he ascended there from this point onward, he remained forty days and forty nights — the days of the formation of a fetus — in order to acquire beneath it an esteemed apprehension of His Being (הֲוָיָה), worthy of being heard from the mouth of the Master such as none other than he could attain, as he testified in saying: כִּי קָרַן עוֹר פָּנָיו בְּדַבְּרוֹ אִתּוֹ (Exodus 34:29). Their sin spoiled this at the end of the first forty days, at the time when it was fitting to attain it, as it says: לֶךְ רֵד כִּי שִׁחֵת עַמְּךָ (Exodus 32:7). In the middle period, according to the received tradition, they were in wrath and did not merit to benefit from the beams of glory; and this was attained in the last forty days, during which he was commanded concerning the work of the Tabernacle, as He explained in saying: וְאֶל הָאָרֹן תִּתֵּן אֶת הָעֵדֻת אֲשֶׁר אֶתֵּן אֵלֶיךָ (Exodus 25:21). This was not fulfilled with the first tablets, which came to no ark at all but were broken, without testimony, as the Sages of blessed memory said: the tablets were broken and the letters flew off. This itself He explained in saying: וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְתוֹכָם (Exodus 25:8) — not as He had promised beforehand, as it says: מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי בְכָל הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַזְכִּיר אֶת שְׁמִי אָבֹא אֵלֶיךָ (Exodus 20:21); but now priests would be required. This itself was explained in saying: וְאַתָּה הַקְרֵב אֵלֶיךָ אֶת אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ (Exodus 28:1). And indeed the tribe of Levi was not chosen to minister until after the matter of the calf, as He testified in saying: בָּעֵת הַהִיא הִבְדִּיל ה' אֶת שֵׁבֶט הַלֵּוִי לְשָׁרְתוֹ וּלְבָרֵךְ בִּשְׁמוֹ (Deuteronomy 10:8). He said: if so, that every time Moses ascended the mountain he stayed forty days and forty nights, and the occasion on which this end was attained was the last of them all — in which he was commanded concerning the work of the Tabernacle. And after he finished the work of the Tabernacle and the priestly garments and the incense and the anointing oil, he explained that at the end of the first period the blessed El gave the first tablets, and the Blessed One caused no impediment whatsoever — for לֹא עִנָּה מִלִּבּוֹ (Lamentations 3:33) — but rather Israel corrupted their own matter, as it says: כִּי שִׁחֵת עַמְּךָ (Exodus 32:7). In the middle period, according to the tradition of our Rabbis of blessed memory, was the passage רְאֵה אַתָּה אֹמֵר אֵלַי (Exodus 33:12) and פְסָל לְךָ (Exodus 34:1); and in the third was the entire matter that he recounted in saying: וַיְהִי שָׁם עִם ה' אַרְבָּעִים יוֹם וְאַרְבָּעִים לַיְלָה (Exodus 34:28), וַיִּכְתֹּב עַל הַלֻּחֹת (Exodus 34:28) — and he then came down with beams of glory, and was commanded concerning the work of the Tabernacle."], :book :exodus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "הֲוָיָה here refers to apprehension of divine Being/essence; kept in Hebrew as Sforno's philosophical term. 'Middle period' (בָּאֶמְצָעִיִּים) refers to the second set of forty days according to rabbinic tradition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:27:19.781070887Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}